#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 600.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153168} :activity/id #uuid "2d6eb053-cae1-5c17-b076-ba71324db2b8" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049408 :topic/id #uuid "f1e8e720-50ea-5aa2-9e19-afac3ed8baad" :topic/math-academy-id 2738} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153168 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153410 :tutorial/content "Let $A$ be an  $m \\times m$ matrix. Recall that a nonzero vector $\\mathbf{v}_1$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda$ if\n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{v}_1 = \\lambda \\mathbf{v}_1.\n$$\n\nWe can write this equation as \n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I) \\mathbf{v}_1 = \\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix $A-\\lambda I$ maps the vector $\\mathbf{v}_1$ to the zero vector $\\mathbf{0}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}_1 \\xrightarrow{A- \\lambda I} \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nNow suppose that there exists a nonzero vector $\\mathbf v_2$ that is mapped to the zero vector in *two* steps by the matrix $A-\\lambda I.$ That is,\n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\mathbf v_2 = \\mathbf v_1 \\qquad \\textrm{and}\\qquad (A-\\lambda I)\\mathbf v_1 = \\mathbf 0,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf v_1$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda.$\n\nWe can represent this schematically as a chain of mappings, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}_2 \\xrightarrow{A- \\lambda I} \\mathbf{v}_{1} \\xrightarrow{A- \\lambda I}  \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nWe call $\\mathbf v_2$ a **generalized eigenvector of rank** $2$ because it is mapped to the zero vector in two steps. \n\nBy the same idea, an eigenvector of $A$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ because it is mapped to the zero vector in one step." :tutorial/id #uuid "88994c46-d16a-531c-895a-e2c3fdddfc1f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5419 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "70c56d2e-edef-5c66-b434-a79d78754fe4" :step/math-academy-id 33794 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153172}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153169 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152224 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152034 :question/id #uuid "3d5ffe46-c0ac-5309-a31b-28a6a1d927e5" :question/math-academy-id "e-9054"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a0abad78-e492-5d09-a04d-bce54605c78c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050857}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152380} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152383} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244298} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186246999} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247000} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362675} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412999} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433716} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433717} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433718}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Generalized Eigenvectors of a 2x2 Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "3ba3a9ff-1652-5e2f-a17b-31f53f5fa7ce" :step/math-academy-id 33795 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153173}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153170 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152225 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152035 :question/id #uuid "30daf182-0ed6-5ddd-994e-a5af7c6c6c01" :question/math-academy-id "e-9056"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "04926916-8e08-5401-a57b-3fd333741913" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152379} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152382} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244251} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244253} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244262} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244274} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244291} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244292} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362661} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412998} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413000} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433719}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Generalized Eigenvectors Using Definition"} :step/id #uuid "4c7afd25-604b-599f-93ac-e074a6562587" :step/math-academy-id 33799 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153171}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153171 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152226 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152036 :question/id #uuid "79ffef9f-8be0-581f-b2f1-dc796d2cf94b" :question/math-academy-id "e-9057"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "76e60bc4-ae61-5c39-ad80-14e0cdab14da" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152381} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152384} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247001} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247002} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247011} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247012} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247013} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247016} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247254} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362682} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433715}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Generalized Eigenvectors of a 3x3 Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "c580f07b-112f-562a-a89e-5d7287e732f7" :step/math-academy-id 33800} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153172 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153411 :tutorial/content "Consider the matrix $A$ and the vector $\\mathbf v,$ given by\n![](images/86/866c306428d7726f8078af9f608a839d6f4971a4a3e368e84d023503ea22ba82.svg)\nThe matrix $A$ has only one eigenvalue, namely $\\lambda = 3.$ Let's show that $\\mathbf v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to this eigenvalue.\n\n- First, we compute $A-3I{:}$\n![](images/b7/b7bcc3d7905efb5425f9ad2e840d574a0c613b3fbdd65742b24608d8f17fcf1b.svg)\n\n- Next, we find the image of $\\mathbf{v}$ under the action of $A-3I{:}$\n![](images/df/df135de6113520b548112c1b8cd9f77d51d99c9b1c5ee3d35f0ab4ce1f6f500e.svg)\n\n- Since we got a nonzero vector in the last step, we now compute the image of $\\mathbf{w}$ under the action of $A-3I{:}$\n![](images/d2/d24081710386fa3719b171bc47f5d10ff12ddecdd0f9fa5da6d5ef4926b9c3e3.svg)\n\nSo, we obtain the following chain of maps:\n\n![](images/90/9041ec615dce97097d94d895e6668e4b97096fd91ad47947fac7f7841c739c9d.svg)\n\nAs we can see,  $A-3I$ maps $\\mathbf{v}$ to $\\mathbf{0}$ in $2$ steps.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $\\mathbf{v}$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=3$ of $A.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "398d8883-81a4-5080-8b00-904f74cdef07" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5587 :tutorial/title "An Example of a Generalized Eigenvector of Rank Two for a 2x2 Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "3ec840df-f049-5126-988c-9c7bbb99172d" :step/math-academy-id 34485 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153169}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153173 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153412 :tutorial/content "Let $A$ be an  $m \\times m$ matrix. A **generalized eigenvector of rank $n$** corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda$ of  $A$ is a nonzero vector $\\mathbf{v}_n$ such that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}_n \\xrightarrow{A- \\lambda I} \\mathbf{v}_{n-1} \\xrightarrow{A- \\lambda I} \\ldots \\xrightarrow{A- \\lambda I} \\mathbf{v}_1 \\xrightarrow{A- \\lambda I} \\mathbf{0},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{v}_{n-1}, \\mathbf{v}_{n-2},\\ldots, \\mathbf{v}_{1} \\neq \\mathbf{0}.$ In other words, $A-\\lambda I$ maps $\\mathbf{v}$ to $\\mathbf{0}$ in $n$ steps." :tutorial/id #uuid "6382bc76-a365-5639-83d8-91bd8605500a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5588 :tutorial/title "Generalized Eigenvectors of Rank N"} :step/id #uuid "cd7d895d-dd6d-5fb6-acf9-cde71c0edb5f" :step/math-academy-id 34486 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153170}}] :activity/title "Generalized Eigenvectors" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186152358}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046673} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413002} :activity/id #uuid "b5cf7ec7-b0e0-5d92-bb87-56ae58980cda" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049408 :topic/id #uuid "f1e8e720-50ea-5aa2-9e19-afac3ed8baad" :topic/math-academy-id 2738} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413001 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413000 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c6b747f5-b6fd-5eba-ae77-a9174a9eb0e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453252} {:answer/id #uuid "52b890bf-1e84-54cf-9889-91651247a068" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453253}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453253} :answer-field/id #uuid "43c1092e-91a4-5e66-9c90-37891305f270" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454376} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fc93c4a8-f3e6-52d8-9ef2-057cd1fd00b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453254} {:answer/id #uuid "9e356dd9-cc07-5fef-886f-0f4115f8b97a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453255}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453254} :answer-field/id #uuid "4825cadc-9f85-54bd-99c2-cb4b67256536" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454377} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "604f4f38-dfc8-500b-b033-c1d586698b9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453256} {:answer/id #uuid "00d18245-d9fc-5c4f-8e90-f44ae94f6e70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453257}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453256} :answer-field/id #uuid "205d0d73-77db-5f0e-9eb3-e7a3ed04c9e4" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454378}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "eddb46c6-735a-5bdf-b2a2-b57344c5441a" :question/math-academy-id "q-109434" :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{aligned}s\\overset{A-4I\\,}{\\to }u\\overset{A-4I\\,}{\\to }v\\overset{A-4I\\,}{\\to }w\\overset{A-4I\\,}{\\to }0 \\\\ x\\overset{\\,\\,\\,A\\,\\,\\,\\,}{\\to }y\\overset{\\,\\,\\,A\\,\\,\\,\\,}{\\to }0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the action of the matrices $A-4I$ and $A$ on the nonzero vectors $s,u,v,w,x$ and $y$ as depicted above, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $s$ is a generalized  eigenvector of rank $4$  corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =4$ of $A$\n- **II.** $x$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ of $A$\n- **III.** $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =4$ of $A$" :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{n}$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{n}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{n-1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }…\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{n-1},{v}_{n-2},…,{v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since \n\n$$\ns\\overset{A-4I\\,}{\\to }u\\overset{A-4I\\,}{\\to }v\\overset{A-4I\\,}{\\to }w\\overset{A-4I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A-4I$ maps $s$ to $0$ in $4$ steps, \nthe vector $s$ is an generalized eigenvector of rank $4$  corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =4$ of $A.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since\n\n$$\nx\\overset{\\,\\,\\,A\\,\\,\\,\\,}{\\to }y\\overset{\\,\\,\\,A\\,\\,\\,\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A=A-0I$ maps $x$ to $0$ in $2$ steps, \nthe vector $x$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2,$ not $1.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since\n\n$$\nv\\overset{A-4I\\,}{\\to }w\\overset{A-4I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A-4I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $2$ steps, \nthe vector $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2,$ not $3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} :step/id #uuid "2bc8515b-cb7e-52c2-b2ca-3e1502bf57b3" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413003}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413002 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412998 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8610a0d2-fdc1-5ffb-bb7d-fa4d5ec60050" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453246} {:answer/id #uuid "1106162e-004a-5376-84ea-4d09f59735a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453247}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453247} :answer-field/id #uuid "16308504-1737-59ec-85f8-6aae9a6e62ca" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454373} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "654b12c7-247f-59a8-b59b-c9aa7e6df741" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453248} {:answer/id #uuid "d1caa7bb-f241-5fda-a091-982727ce423c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453249}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453248} :answer-field/id #uuid "dec4b167-b790-52bb-b09f-3c495acb1957" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454374} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7fb38f24-ce58-5679-8e4c-5ab1475e60b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453250} {:answer/id #uuid "c9beb16d-fb59-5d8a-9e62-f88859d6a643" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453251}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453251} :answer-field/id #uuid "291c74d0-3740-5390-9af2-8599a8ba49e9" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454375}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "28941d92-3462-55d4-9376-780e240021c7" :question/math-academy-id "q-109277" :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }v\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }w\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }0 \\\\ x\\overset{A-3I\\,}{\\to }y\\overset{A-3I\\,}{\\to }0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the action of the matrices $A+3I$ and $A-3I$ on the nonzero vectors $u,v,w,x$ and $y$ as depicted above, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3$ of $A$\n\n- **II.** $u$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3$ of $A$\n\n- **III.** $y$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3$ of $A$" :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{n}$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{n}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{n-1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }…\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{n-1},{v}_{n-2},…,{v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since \n\n$$\nv\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }w\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A+3I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $2$ steps, \nthe vector $v$ is a generalized  eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3$ of $A.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since\n\n$$\nu\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }v\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }w\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A+3I$ maps $u$ to $0$ in $3$ steps, \nthe vector $u$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3,$ not $2.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since\n\n$$\ny\\overset{A-3I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A-3I$ maps $y$ to $0$ in $1$ step, \nthe vector $y$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3$ of $A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "ad6297d7-7ae6-5be0-a908-e7a0cc46d152" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413001}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413003 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412999 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "787f9f37-580f-5658-9df9-ce826b611451" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472496} {:answer/id #uuid "c4403496-69e1-5d08-b02d-1b03668dd8c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472497}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186472496} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f1a3fb6-945f-5879-9865-fdc5a922c3f4" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473643} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7460c1a4-dd63-595d-8732-ee34d9a2c09a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472498} {:answer/id #uuid "71f5c96a-5c5b-5605-9ce0-b480c9528139" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472499}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186472498} :answer-field/id #uuid "a767f879-2f51-5809-aebb-daff65b44383" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473644} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a97ed2f-d3d1-546a-874d-42ae2f24fc40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472500} {:answer/id #uuid "be2ff147-060f-5c7b-81e0-38256805dec8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472501}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186472501} :answer-field/id #uuid "b3046741-713a-58b0-a1a8-53bf395bbfbb" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473645}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5e5966d3-ce4d-5835-ab91-1f478e587602" :question/math-academy-id "q-109510" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $v$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** $v$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of $A$\n- **II.** $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of $A$\n- **III.** $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of $A$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{2}$ of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ Recall that an *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nFirst, we compute $A-2I:$\n\n$$\nA-2I=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the image of $v$ under the action of $A-2I:$\n\n$$\n(A-2I)v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Since we got a nonzero vector in the last step, we now compute the image of $w$ under the action of $A-2I:$\n\n$$\n(A-2I)w=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=0\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the following chain of maps:\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{v}\\overset{\\,A-2I\\,\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\overset{\\,A-2I\\,\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{0}\n$$\n\nHere, we see that $A-2I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $2$ steps.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$\\,$ *$v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of $A.$*\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "ea9a4e19-af76-5650-86a8-7d1214ec77ba"}] :activity/title "Generalized Eigenvectors" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412979}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436621} :activity/id #uuid "a709859d-ebd8-5943-9d11-a9e7d7df3f7b" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049408 :topic/id #uuid "f1e8e720-50ea-5aa2-9e19-afac3ed8baad" :topic/math-academy-id 2738} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436617 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247013 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "88e6960e-31f3-5ac1-82cd-c838adf3ce80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461599} {:answer/id #uuid "a7f3d6b5-109b-55a8-9d7e-93a79b370e1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461600}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186461599} :answer-field/id #uuid "cdf5a34e-d7b9-5239-b72f-eb88fc65dd7f" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462553} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "50d0d8ce-4f3b-5424-bf07-bdfe2235c2c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461601} {:answer/id #uuid "e38713c4-190c-53e7-b2aa-ba96f128d161" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461602}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186461601} :answer-field/id #uuid "5da6cb28-852f-52a7-94cd-60fc2ff5a4f5" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462554} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ba3f91d-4472-57c6-b55c-6bc75a7ae7ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461603} {:answer/id #uuid "92e3ed04-c863-5644-a051-7573bdb252b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461604}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186461604} :answer-field/id #uuid "dfa287d1-edf5-5f1b-a9c9-98b1433d7624" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462555}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "6ee1784f-5074-5474-b955-50f16f51c21c" :question/math-academy-id "q-109626" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -5 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -9 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $v$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $v$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2$ of $A$\n\nII. $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2$ of $A$\n\nIII. $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2$ of $A$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{n}$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{n}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{n-1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }…\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{n-1},{v}_{n-2},…,{v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nFirst, we compute $A+2I:$\n\n$$\nA+2I=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -5 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -9 & 1\\end{aligned}]+2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -9 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the image of $v$ under the action of $A+2I:$\n\n$$\n(A+2I)v=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -9 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Since we got a nonzero vector, we now compute the image of $w$ under the action of $A+2I:$\n\n$$\n(A+2I)w=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -9 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Again, since we got a nonzero vector in the last step, we now compute the image of $x$ under the action of $A+2I:$\n\n$$\n(A+2I)x=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -9 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=0\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the following chain of maps:\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{v}\\overset{A+2I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\overset{A+2I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}\\overset{A+2I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{0}\n$$\n\nHere, we see that $A+2I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $3$ steps.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$\\,$ *$v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2$ of $A.$*\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "33739050-145e-5afa-87ac-beb7499e226a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436618}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436618 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247016 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e1cd008-4fbf-50fc-9e43-bc6b7e21f011" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461605} {:answer/id #uuid "1912e2b9-28ee-5c97-8288-1089044dd3d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461606}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186461605} :answer-field/id #uuid "4307f5cf-d0aa-5fb8-b7b7-d67a11c1f175" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462556} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39a5a3d4-6d1a-5629-b7b1-6f8ec7b3cad3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461607} {:answer/id #uuid "01e67c93-8273-5547-baa2-346b02e1be44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461608}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186461607} :answer-field/id #uuid "9b78fde5-c14f-557d-8822-beab3bfcb7a5" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462557} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e34478a7-ae4f-5812-b252-95716506507b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461609} {:answer/id #uuid "90ea6604-ce8c-5eff-9548-1b53c84aafc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461610}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186461609} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d3b0db8-22db-57a0-a1c9-2f3066587f31" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462558}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "73154a72-4618-59f1-ab95-d4b82ca2da71" :question/math-academy-id "q-109632" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $v$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $v$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3$ of $A$\n\nII. $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3$ of $A$\n\nIII. $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3$ of $A$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{n}$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{n}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{n-1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }…\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{n-1},{v}_{n-2},…,{v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nFirst, we compute $A-3I:$\n\n$$\nA-3I=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the image of $v$ under the action of $A-3I:$\n\n$$\n(A-3I)v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Since we got a nonzero vector, we now compute the image of $w$ under the action of $A-3I:$\n\n$$\n(A-3I)w=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Again, since we got a nonzero vector in the last step, we now compute the image of $x$ under the action of $A-3I:$\n\n$$\n(A-3I)x=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=0\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the following chain of maps:\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{v}\\overset{A-3I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\overset{A-3I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}\\overset{A-3I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{0}\n$$\n\nHere, we see that $A-3I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $3$ steps.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$\\,$ *$v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3$ of $A.$*\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\""} :step/id #uuid "4ffd44fb-40d1-534f-9793-15a7bf6ec9e8"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436619 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247002 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b486bce2-fa4e-513e-b610-7ee71b9233d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461581} {:answer/id #uuid "c967c74a-a0ee-5cb2-add3-5fb704306c38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461582}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186461581} :answer-field/id #uuid "2da9584a-9261-5bdd-a02e-94d0f8f396e9" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462544} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "97da4738-01b7-5e07-a794-eb18f27e9287" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461583} {:answer/id #uuid "89d9db19-d46a-585f-b48b-56f4ec6588cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461584}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186461583} :answer-field/id #uuid "34cf3572-0f16-58e0-891b-e7abf95b07a2" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462545} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "378a528d-b550-5f7a-974c-a7e21760d896" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461585} {:answer/id #uuid "0b31d8dd-5d0d-5f38-aee8-7880656f4805" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461586}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186461586} :answer-field/id #uuid "5bcc4b93-4a4f-5d15-a1a8-5b17a48be491" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462546}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1069f3ce-ee29-5917-b7ea-2041ea76b45b" :question/math-academy-id "q-109525" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $v$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $v$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1$ of $A$\n\nII. $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1$ of $A$\n\nIII. $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1$ of $A$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{n}$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{n}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{n-1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }…\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{n-1},{v}_{n-2},…,{v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nFirst, we compute $A-I:$\n\n$$\nA-I=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the image of $v$ under the action of $A-I:$\n\n$$\n(A-I)v=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Since we got a nonzero vector in the last step, we now compute the image of $w$ under the action of $A-I:$\n\n$$\n(A-I)w=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=0\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the following chain of maps:\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{v}\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{0}\n$$\n\nHere, we see that $A-I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $2$ steps.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$\\,$ *$v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1$ of $A.$*\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "5fcf7480-bb6d-551f-bed4-edf70898be3d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436617}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436620 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412998 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8610a0d2-fdc1-5ffb-bb7d-fa4d5ec60050" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453246} {:answer/id #uuid "1106162e-004a-5376-84ea-4d09f59735a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453247}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453247} :answer-field/id #uuid "16308504-1737-59ec-85f8-6aae9a6e62ca" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454373} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "654b12c7-247f-59a8-b59b-c9aa7e6df741" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453248} {:answer/id #uuid "d1caa7bb-f241-5fda-a091-982727ce423c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453249}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453248} :answer-field/id #uuid "dec4b167-b790-52bb-b09f-3c495acb1957" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454374} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7fb38f24-ce58-5679-8e4c-5ab1475e60b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453250} {:answer/id #uuid "c9beb16d-fb59-5d8a-9e62-f88859d6a643" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453251}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453251} :answer-field/id #uuid "291c74d0-3740-5390-9af2-8599a8ba49e9" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454375}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "28941d92-3462-55d4-9376-780e240021c7" :question/math-academy-id "q-109277" :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }v\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }w\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }0 \\\\ x\\overset{A-3I\\,}{\\to }y\\overset{A-3I\\,}{\\to }0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the action of the matrices $A+3I$ and $A-3I$ on the nonzero vectors $u,v,w,x$ and $y$ as depicted above, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3$ of $A$\n\n- **II.** $u$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3$ of $A$\n\n- **III.** $y$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3$ of $A$" :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{n}$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{n}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{n-1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }…\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{n-1},{v}_{n-2},…,{v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since \n\n$$\nv\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }w\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A+3I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $2$ steps, \nthe vector $v$ is a generalized  eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3$ of $A.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since\n\n$$\nu\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }v\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }w\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A+3I$ maps $u$ to $0$ in $3$ steps, \nthe vector $u$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3,$ not $2.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since\n\n$$\ny\\overset{A-3I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A-3I$ maps $y$ to $0$ in $1$ step, \nthe vector $y$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3$ of $A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "af00e671-378f-5ae3-95bf-22f18e729b56" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436619}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436621 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152382 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "76487220-b477-5abf-bad9-0ee00e51ee59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447962} {:answer/id #uuid "81057c8d-5262-5c95-bc7b-4452b2ad2b7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447963}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447963} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb13590c-079b-519c-93ab-906034ba110e" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449021} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c4ddf16-3f07-5026-9fb4-88fb7f2841f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447964} {:answer/id #uuid "dc61d811-4e83-5a14-9444-b8198f3992f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447965}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447965} :answer-field/id #uuid "6ac03e9b-a3c6-5390-a22b-08e7e984714a" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449022} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e675f8e3-aafe-5f61-ab53-bb35d4a720f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447966} {:answer/id #uuid "03029349-ead0-574e-a243-e4d30c60444b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447967}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447966} :answer-field/id #uuid "a3a77f9b-67a0-599f-85f1-4d81bc912a21" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449023}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e4b429ab-49a9-5137-bfd6-13315591e96f" :question/math-academy-id "q-109243" :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\overset{A-5I\\,}{\\to }v\\overset{A-5I\\,}{\\to }w\\overset{A-5I\\,}{\\to }0 \\\\ x\\overset{\\,\\,\\,A\\,\\,\\,\\,}{\\to }y\\overset{\\,\\,\\,A\\,\\,\\,\\,}{\\to }0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the action of the matrices $A-5I$ and $A$ on the nonzero vectors $u,v,w,x$ and $y$ as depicted above, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $v$ is a generalized  eigenvector of rank $2$  corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =5$ of $A$\n\n- **II.** $y$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ of $A$\n\n- **III.** $u$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $4$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =5$ of $A$" :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{n}$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{n}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{n-1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }…\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{n-1},{v}_{n-2},…,{v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since \n\n$$\nv\\overset{A-5I\\,}{\\to }w\\overset{A-5I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A-5I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $2$ steps, \nthe vector $v$ is an generalized eigenvector of rank $2$  corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =5$ of $A.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, since\n\n$$\ny\\overset{\\,\\,\\,A\\,\\,\\,\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A=A-0I$ maps $y$ to $0$ in $1$ step, \nthe vector $y$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ (a.k.a. an eigenvector) corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ of $A.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since\n\n$$\nu\\overset{A-5I\\,}{\\to }v\\overset{A-5I\\,}{\\to }w\\overset{A-5I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A-5I$ maps $u$ to $0$ in $3$ steps, \nthe vector $u$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3,$ not $4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "f6d6dca3-2180-5d58-90e6-5c5ae7a5a3ef" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436620}}] :activity/title "Generalized Eigenvectors" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436616}]}
